(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-lab-diagnostics-quiz"]={"title":"Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics Quiz 1 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"During which phase of testing do most errors occur?","choices":["Errors are distributed evenly across the three phases","The pretest phase","The intratest phase","The posttest phase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and this is why preparation, labeling and communication receive so much attention. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Why is a tongue blade used during a throat culture?","choices":["To collect the specimen directly from the surface of the tongue where organisms accumulate","To keep the patient from swallowing during collection","To protect the clinician from exposure","To increase visualization of the pharynx, relax the throat muscles and decrease the gag reflex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three purposes are named. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which test is listed among common point-of-care tests in the acute care setting?","choices":["Fecal occult blood","Cholesterol","Troponin","Hemoglobin A1c"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside venous blood gas, point-of-care glucose, brain natriuretic peptide and D-dimer. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why does knowing the pre-test probability help before a test is ordered?","choices":["It indicates the chance the condition is present beforehand, which helps decide whether the test is worth ordering at all","It allows the sensitivity and specificity of the test to be adjusted for that particular patient before it is ordered","It determines which collection tube should be used","It establishes whether the test requires a laboratory certificate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that judgement comes from the patient's presentation and risk factors. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why does understanding a patient's perceptions and anxieties about testing matter?","choices":["It allows communication on a deeper level so that a therapeutic relationship can develop","It allows the clinician to decide whether to disclose the result","It determines whether consent is legally required","It identifies which patients should be referred to another clinician before any testing begins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Empathy is expected throughout all phases of testing. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which organism is a throat culture most often obtained to identify?","choices":["Candida albicans, because of the risk of thrush","Streptococci, because of the risk of beta-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis","Staphylococcus aureus, because of the risk of toxin-mediated illness","Haemophilus influenzae, because of the risk of epiglottitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — most commonly affecting children aged three to fifteen years. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which characteristic belongs to quantitative testing?","choices":["Data are numbers or statistical results, analysed statistically","Data are descriptions requiring non-statistical grouping","Results are always reported as positive or negative","Results require no instrumentation to interpret"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — quantitative testing measures and tests, answering how many or how much. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Before ordering a test, which two questions should be asked about it?","choices":["Will it support or guide management, and is it cost effective?","Is it available in-house, and how quickly does it result?","Has the patient had it before, and did they tolerate it?","Is it the newest available assay, and is it automated?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — appropriateness for this patient is the first consideration, not availability. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which complications should be monitored for during the posttest phase?","choices":["Only adverse effects of sedation","Only laboratory errors in the reported value, since the physical risks have passed once the test is complete","Bleeding, infection, respiratory difficulties, perforation, and adverse effects of sedation or anesthesia","Only bleeding at the puncture site"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — these five are the named posttest complications. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which principle applies to throat, sputum and blood cultures alike?","choices":["Collect three separate specimens on consecutive days","Send the specimen only once the patient has become febrile","Obtain the specimen before initiating antibiotics whenever possible","Refrigerate the specimen immediately after collection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — stated for all three, since antibiotics reduce the yield. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are the three phases of the diagnostic testing process, in order?","choices":["Preanalytical, postanalytical, analytical","Screening, diagnostic, confirmatory","Collection, transport, interpretation","Pretest, intratest, posttest"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — also called the preanalytical, analytical and postanalytical phases. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A significant abnormal result returns. Which posttest responsibility does this trigger?","choices":["Identify and treat critical values, and communicate results clearly and sensitively","Repeat the test in the central laboratory before informing the patient of anything","Defer communication until the follow-up appointment","Refer immediately without interpreting the result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Behavioural responses to a significant diagnosis may last several weeks or longer. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How large a stool sample should be applied to a guaiac card?","choices":["The sample size does not affect interpretation","A small sample — a large one obscures the result","A large sample, to maximise sensitivity","Enough to fill the window completely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. More is not better here. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which is listed as a limitation of point-of-care testing?","choices":["The need for venipuncture rather than fingerstick","Requirement for a hospital setting","Operator and manufacturer variability","Longer turnaround than central laboratory testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside expense, difficulty controlling quality assurance, non-standardised vocabulary, imprecision and supply needs. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A test with 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity is used in a high-prevalence population and then in a low-prevalence one. What happens to positive predictive value?","choices":["It cannot be compared without recalculating sensitivity","It is higher in the high-prevalence population and lower in the low-prevalence one","It stays the same, because the test characteristics are unchanged","It is lower where prevalence is high, because more true cases dilute the result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the frostbite example gives 68% in the high-prevalence setting against about 2% in the low-prevalence one, with the same test. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments program establish?","choices":["Voluntary best-practice recommendations that apply to hospital laboratories but not to other sites","Maximum permissible turnaround times for laboratory results","Reimbursement rates for laboratory testing","Federal guidelines for minimum quality standards when testing human samples at all types of sites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it sets the floor, and it arose from problems in cytology laboratories reading Papanicolaou smears. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which characteristics describe a screening test?","choices":["Typically performed in order to confirm a condition that is already strongly suspected","Typically reserved for symptomatic patients","Typically inexpensive and easy to perform, indicating whether further testing is needed","Typically invasive with a meaningful risk of complications"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — screening should be performed before more expensive or time-consuming tests. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A urine pregnancy test returns simply positive or negative. How is this analyser type classified?","choices":["Qualitative","Quantitative","Semi-quantitative","Multiple-use benchtop"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — rapid strep and influenza tests are likewise qualitative; a urinalysis dipstick is semi-quantitative. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"When does the pretest phase begin and end?","choices":["It begins when the specimen is collected and ends when it is analysed","It begins with patient preparation and extends until the diagnostic test begins","It begins when the order is placed and ends when the specimen reaches the laboratory","It begins at the patient's arrival and ends when consent is signed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — preparation through to the moment the test starts. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a variable that can affect laboratory results?","choices":["The ordering clinician's specialty","Whether the test was ordered electronically","Time of specimen collection","The patient's insurance carrier"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The list also includes patient preparation, current drug therapy, physical activity, hydration, age, sex and body mass index. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does sensitivity measure?","choices":["The probability that a person with a negative test lacks the disease","The probability that the test is positive when the person does have the condition","The probability that the test is negative when the person does not have the condition","The probability that a person with a positive test has the disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it reflects how well a test detects a condition, and relates to fewer false negatives. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What defines non-instrumental point-of-care testing?","choices":["It produces a numerical result without any reader device","It does not rely on instrumentation to interpret results","It requires no reagents or consumables","It may only be performed by licensed personnel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — urine pregnancy testing, coronavirus tests and fecal occult blood are the examples. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which agency categorizes tests based on complexity under the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments?","choices":["The Food and Drug Administration","The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services","The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","The Joint Commission"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it also reviews waiver applications and develops categorization guidance. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is pre-test probability?","choices":["The likelihood that the test will be performed correctly","The proportion of positive results a laboratory reports","The likelihood of the condition after the result is known, adjusted for the sensitivity and specificity of the assay that produced it","The likelihood of the condition before the test result is known, based on signs, symptoms, history, risk factors and how common it is in the population"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — post-test probability is the likelihood after the result, and depends on sensitivity and specificity. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the purpose of the clear tube in the order of draw?","choices":["It is the tube used for trace metal studies where contamination must be avoided","It is the tube used for blood cultures","It is the serum separator tube","It is a discard tube used to fill collection set spaces before the coagulation tube"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is nonadditive and is used when no royal blue tube is collected. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient presents with an acute febrile illness and suspected septicemia. How should blood cultures be collected?","choices":["A single large-volume sample drawn from either arm before antibiotics are started","Two samples from the same arm, thirty minutes apart","A single sample drawn after the first antibiotic dose","Two separate samples from opposite arms, ideally before starting antibiotic therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the aerobic sample is drawn first. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How does handheld point-of-care equipment differ from a benchtop device?","choices":["Handheld equipment requires a laboratory certificate and trained operators, whereas benchtop devices may be run by anyone on site without additional certification or documented competency","Handheld equipment is easy to carry, involves one to two steps, and usually gives one or few data points; benchtop devices are stationary, multi-step, and give multiple data points","Handheld equipment requires no reagents while benchtop devices do","Handheld equipment is quantitative while benchtop devices are qualitative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — portability, complexity and data yield all differ. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which feature of point-of-care testing is described as universal and inherent?","choices":["Results should be more precise than central laboratory methods","It should be performed only by licensed laboratory personnel","It should replace central laboratory testing wherever available","Results should align with established laboratory methods"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside simplicity of use, durable reagents, and safety during testing. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Screening for human immunodeficiency virus uses a highly sensitive test first. Why is this the right order?","choices":["A highly sensitive test provides a definitive diagnosis on its own","Confirmatory testing is unnecessary once a sensitive screen is negative","It is better to have a few false positives, later corrected by a confirmatory test, than to miss infected individuals who might unknowingly infect others","A highly sensitive test costs less than a highly specific one in essentially every case, so it is the appropriate first step whenever budgets are constrained"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the confirmatory supplemental test is highly specific and minimises false-positive diagnoses. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which of the following is a named quality assurance measure for point-of-care testing?","choices":["Active enrollment and participation in an External Quality Assurance program","Annual replacement of every device regardless of its measured performance","Testing only specimens collected by laboratory staff","Reporting results verbally rather than electronically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside trained and competent operators, correct specimen collection, accurate patient identification and quality control. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
